00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.220
I'm Roop Kiran Chevuri,
Principal Group Product Manager

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.920
for Windows 365 and
Azure Virtual Desktop.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:11.700
PHIL GERITY: And
I'm Phil Gerity,

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.460
Partner Group Product
Manager for Windows 365

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.140
and Azure Virtual Desktop.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.040
ROOP KIRAN CHEVURI:
Together we'll walk you

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.950
through how Windows 365 is
being designed and evolved

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:22.980
to support modern developers,
whether you're building locally,

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.890
in the cloud, or across
hybrid environments.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.770
Let's take a quick look at
what we will cover today.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:29.880
In this session, we are going

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.430
to show how Windows 365
helps you build, test,

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:36.060
and scale faster, from reducing
environment setup friction

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:39.450
to enabling AI and agent-driven
workflows, all on a secure,

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:42.090
centrally managed foundation.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:43.740
Developers face new challenges.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.830
Let's take a look at
what Windows 365 enables

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.060
for developers today.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:51.210
If you are a developer, this
probably sounds familiar.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.310
You sit down, ready
to write code,

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:56.130
and instead you are dealing
with environment setup,

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:57.690
configuration
issues, or switching

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.000
between machines and platforms.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.250
As development environments
continue to evolve,

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:03.840
they are also becoming
more complex,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.330
and that complexity is
pulling developers away

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:07.860
from the work they
actually want to do,

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.720
which is building software.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.550
The data reflects this clearly.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.520
More than half of developers'
time is now spent

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.000
on maintenance, configuration,
and operational tasks,

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.920
rather than writing new code.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.340
Over 75% of developers need
secured virtualization

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.620
or container-based
environments just

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.450
to do their day-to-day work.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:31.080
And 40% or more need
cross-platform access,

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:35.040
often moving between Windows,
macOS, and Linux to build, test,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.810
and validate applications.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.420
These are the increasing
challenges for developers,

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.920
and it's this context for why we
started rethinking how Windows

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.110
development environments
should work.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.570
This is where Windows
365 comes in.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.660
Microsoft is uniquely
positioned to bring the PC

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.010
and the cloud together,

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:56.580
and Windows 365 delivers
those benefits directly

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.380
to modern development.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:00.900
It provides a cloud
development environment

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:04.320
with a ready-to-code
Windows 365 cloud PC,

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.510
which is pre-configured
with GitHub

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.250
and preferred developer tools,

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.530
helping teams get
productive faster

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:14.100
and reduce environment friction.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.670
Just as Office 365 brought
productivity to the cloud,

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.370
Windows 365 brings
Windows to the cloud.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:24.120
For you, this means the right
environment for each task.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.030
You can assign one or
multiple cloud PCs,

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:30.000
each set up for a specific
workload, whether for coding,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.820
testing, or AI development.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:36.900
With Windows App, you can access
your cloud PC from any device

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.510
and switch seamlessly
between your local desktop

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:45.060
and cloud environment, using
familiar gestures and shortcuts.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.800
Windows 365 securely streams a
full Windows experience, apps,

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.250
data, and settings from
Microsoft Cloud to any device,

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:57.000
giving you consistent, secured
access without being constrained

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.720
by local hardware or
configuration issues.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.160
With Windows 365, you can focus
less on managing environments

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:09.120
and more on coding and shipping.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.470
So far, we talked about how
Windows 365 helps developers

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:16.350
work with less friction,
but development isn't just

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.270
about human developers anymore.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.430
We are seeing a
fundamental shift

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:22.950
in how software gets built,

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:26.640
where developers are no longer
working alone but with AI

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.050
and agents that generate code,
test ideas, automate tasks,

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:33.900
and unlock entirely
new workflows.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.450
And whether the work is
done by people or by AI

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.250
and agents acting
on their behalf,

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:41.820
enterprises still require
a secure foundation

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:46.380
with centralized IT control
as a non-negotiable baseline.

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:48.270
This is where Windows
365 becomes more

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.040
than just a development
environment;

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.230
it becomes a platform.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:53.220
In the rest of the session,

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.920
we'll see how Windows
365 empowers developers

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.330
to experiment, build, and ship
faster while enabling the secure

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:03.780
and scalable environment as
a foundation for IT-driven

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.210
and agentic workflows
to run safely at scale.

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:12.390
Let's start with what Windows
365 can do for human developers.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:14.400
Development today isn't
tied to one machine.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.620
You switch between
devices all day,

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.560
but every switch
comes with friction.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.740
Rebuilding your environment,
syncing your setup,

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:24.540
thereby losing your momentum.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.220
What you really
want is a simple,

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:28.440
same development
environment everywhere.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.990
That's where Windows
365 comes in.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:33.810
You can connect to your full
development environment

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:37.350
as a cloud PC from any
device and pick up right

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:40.410
where you left off
while your code, tools,

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:43.690
and configurations stay
secured in the cloud.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:47.700
Here I am on my
personal Mac device,

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:51.060
but I'm connecting straight
into my development cloud PC.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:54.240
So I still get the full Windows
development experience while my

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.990
source code and environment stay
securely in the cloud instead

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:00.120
of living on the local device.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:03.240
And if you're a developer
working across different kinds

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:05.880
of tasks, you're not
limited to just one setup.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:07.890
You can have multiple
environments,

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:12.900
workload-specific compute needs,
might have a 16-core cloud PC

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:15.030
for your day-to-day coding,
builds, and testing,

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:18.510
and a separate GPU-enabled Cloud
PC when you need more power

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:22.740
for things like heavier compute
or visualization workloads.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:26.010
Getting into Cloud PC is simple,
quick, and frictionless.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:29.580
One click in the Windows App
and you are in, ready to work.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:31.920
It's fast enough to feel
like a seamless extension

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.740
of your workflow and
not a separate step.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:35.880
I'm excited to announce

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:39.690
that Windows 365 now supports
a new Windows 11 developer

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:42.180
configuration image
in public preview.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:45.180
Once the developer-optimized
image Cloud PC launches,

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.070
I'm not starting from
a vanilla machine

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:48.840
or spending time
setting things up.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:51.810
When I sign in, the
environment is ready to go.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:56.640
Tools like Visual Studio, Git,
GitHub CLI, Python, Node.js,

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:58.620
and GitHub Copilot are
already installed,

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:01.170
and WSL with Ubuntu
is already set up.

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:04.170
So I can start work
across both Windows

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.930
and Linux workflows right away.

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:09.150
It also comes with
developer-focused settings

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:11.460
and Windows configurations
already in place,

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:14.130
which helps cut down the
setup friction and gets me

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:19.080
to actual coding and that
first commit a lot faster.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.810
Now, as a developer, I need an
environment that's tailored

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:23.130
to the project I'm working on.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:24.510
And with Cloud PCs,

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:27.930
that environment can be fully
customized with the exact SDKs,

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:31.530
CLIs, and packages, and build
tools my project requires while

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:34.470
still staying within the
enterprise guardrails.

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:37.530
Here, my project needs
WinGet, Docker, and Postman,

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:40.110
and these are getting installed
automatically the moment I

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:41.610
log in.

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:44.070
It can clone my repository
automatically as part

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:47.160
of the project setup script, and
my environment is ready for me.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:50.160
Instead of having to wait for IT
to package tools and push them

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:54.090
to a machine, everything I need
is already there from the start,

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:56.820
so I can jump
straight into coding.

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.770
That means I stay productive

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:01.500
in project-specific setups
while still benefiting

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:03.540
from the organization's
consistency,

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:06.540
security, and control at scale.

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:07.710
Developers get the speed

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:10.770
and flexibility while IT
keeps governance and control.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:13.440
That's the balance
Cloud PC unlocks.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.570
Tokens are the digital
currency of AI,

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:18.090
but developers find themselves
hitting budget constraints

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:20.820
on how much they can spend
running the frontier

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:22.800
models online.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:25.980
To address this, it's becoming
more common for applications

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:27.930
to leverage models
running directly

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:30.840
on the developer-controlled
environment, such as Cloud PCs,

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:33.960
where available compute
can be used more flexibly

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:36.840
without incurring additional
token-based costs.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:39.390
In this case, I'm going to
start by going to VS Code

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:42.270
and launch Microsoft Foundry.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:45.030
From the model catalog page,
you can filter on the models

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:48.030
that can run locally, and in
this case, I'm choosing the one

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:50.070
that runs in the CPU only.

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:53.490
To make this demo short,
I'll do a small Qwen model,

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:55.770
but there are many to choose
from, as you can see.

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:57.480
You're only limited
by the amount

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:00.240
of free RAM you have available.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.670
Windows 365 offers a
diversity of SKUs,

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:08.040
right up to 128-gigabit RAM,
powered by our new 32-core SKU.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:11.550
This downloads and installs
the model locally.

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:13.710
Foundry Local has this
concept of a playground,

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:16.590
which gives me a chat
window to test my models.

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:19.680
So here I'm going to ask it to
create a Hello World GUI app

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:24.990
from Windows in Python, with
Windows-sized 400x400 pixels.

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:28.560
Notice how the CPU spikes
as it uses the local model,

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:31.980
but the networking stays the
same, as everything is happening

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:33.960
within the Cloud PC session.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.900
Once the code is ready, we
can save the file, say,

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:41.670
HelloWorld.python, and then
we can go and execute this.

00:08:41.670 --> 00:08:44.670
With that done, let's
try something more fun.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:49.020
I built this local AI
studio using GitHub,

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:50.610
Copilot CLI to experiment

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:53.600
with running language models
locally on my Cloud PC.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:57.110
It worked great, but I
hit a UI flicker bug.

00:08:57.110 --> 00:08:59.090
I gave Copilot
instructions to fix it,

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:01.250
and now I'm validating the fix.

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:03.810
First, I'll trigger
the vision model.

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:07.410
It analyzes an image
and generates a prompt.

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:10.290
This restarts ComfyUI.

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:13.620
This is the engine used for
the AI image generation.

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:15.720
The restart causes the
caption to update,

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:18.270
which is where the
flicker used to happen.

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:21.270
While that is running, I'll
switch over and load one

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:24.810
of the many models Local AI
Studio detected as would work

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:26.610
on this particular Cloud PC.

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:29.430
I'll use this chat feature
here and paste a prompt

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:33.210
about popular seafood
dishes in Seattle,

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:36.930
and you can see it quickly
returns a detailed response.

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:37.950
Now let's try something else.

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:42.060
Now I'll ask to translate that
into Japanese, and it does.

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:43.560
Let's go back to the
image generation,

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:46.770
which we started with, and
you will see ComfyUI has now

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:50.490
restarted, and the vision model
has generated a new prompt.

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:53.490
So I'll accept the defaults and
run the image generation now

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:56.940
with Stable Diffusion 1.5
using the default settings,

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:59.850
and it works, producing
a fresh interpretation

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:02.070
of the original image.

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:05.160
What this really unlocks is
predictable AI economics.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:07.860
With local language models
running on your device

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:11.580
or Cloud PC, you are no longer
paying per token for every task

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:14.280
that can be solved locally,
whether it's coding,

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:17.460
translation, image
generation, or vision.

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:21.480
These experiences run your app
powered by local language models

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:24.510
and without the variability
of Cloud consumption.

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:26.820
So that's how Windows
365 enables developers

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:29.700
to run language models
directly in the Cloud PC.

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:33.570
But what makes it even more
powerful is how the development

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:36.880
workflow continues beyond
a single environment.

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:40.260
Here I am on my Mac
on the left-hand side

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:42.270
of the screen working in GitHub.

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:45.390
On the right is my
developer-optimized Windows 365

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:48.240
Cloud PC launched
from the Windows App.

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:52.410
Fully configured with GitHub
Copilot CLI and ready to go.

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:55.620
I start by enabling remote
sessions on my Cloud PC.

00:10:55.620 --> 00:10:58.110
Once this is enabled,
I can now monitor

00:10:58.110 --> 00:11:02.100
and steer a running GitHub
Copilot CLI session directly

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:03.810
from my device of choice.

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:06.840
Here I prompt Copilot through
the GitHub web interface

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:09.990
to create a GUI app in
Windows using Python

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:12.060
that says "I love my Cloud PC."

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:15.000
As you can see, I'm not
tied to a single device.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:18.060
I can step away from my desk,
even close my laptop lid,

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:21.000
and still stay connected
to what's happening.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:24.240
The session continues to run on
my always available Cloud PC,

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:28.410
so nothing pauses when my
local device goes to sleep.

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:30.210
The code is written and executed

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:33.930
in the Cloud PC environment even
though I initiate everything

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:36.580
from my local device.

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:40.530
This experience works across
devices; whether I start

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:44.430
on a Mac or a PC or a mobile
device, I can keep an eye

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:46.650
on progress from wherever I am.

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:49.530
It also works across my
workspace, supporting Norgate,

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:52.770
Norgate Hub, and
multi-repo sessions,

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:56.310
so it fits however your
project is structured.

00:11:56.310 --> 00:12:00.000
You can also have multiple
agent sessions running at once.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.400
My Cloud PC handles the
execution while I stay

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:05.160
in control, reviewing progress,
approving next steps,

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:10.050
and guiding the outcome without
needing to stay at my desk.

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:11.190
Because everything runs

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:13.440
in a persistent
enterprise-managed Windows

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:16.350
environment, my work
still remains secure,

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:19.620
consistent, and uninterrupted.

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:22.350
Windows 365 brings your
development environment

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:26.040
to the Cloud so you can stay
productive wherever you are

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:28.620
with the same tools,
the same experience,

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:30.930
and no disruption
to how you work.

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:33.780
In summary, Windows 365
gives you a secure,

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:37.650
personalized dev environment
you can access from any device.

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:40.860
It's always ready to code
with pre-configured tools,

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:43.680
easy to customize, and lets you
run your dev or AI workloads

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:46.410
with predictable costs from
any device while keeping your

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:48.840
workflow seamless
between local and Cloud.

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:50.820
Now let me hand over to Phil.

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:51.660
PHIL GERITY: Thank you, Roop.

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:52.260
Hello, everyone.

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:54.420
I'm Phil, and for the next
few minutes, I'll walk you

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:59.850
through how Windows 365 is also
the platform for AI and agents.

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:02.910
Every AI agent builder
hits the same wall.

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:06.570
Your agent can reason, it
can plan, it can call APIs,

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:08.910
but the moment you need
it to actually do work,

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:13.170
open an application, fill out a
form, navigate a legacy system,

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:16.050
use any app that exists
on Windows, you're stuck.

00:13:16.050 --> 00:13:18.210
You need to give your
agent a computer.

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:20.460
That's what Windows
365 for Agents does.

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:22.830
It gives your agents
their own Cloud PC,

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:25.380
full Windows environments,
so they can see,

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:28.890
interact with apps, and operate
the way a person would.

00:13:28.890 --> 00:13:31.380
And today, I'm here to
tell you that Windows 365

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:34.260
for Agents is
generally available.

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:36.510
Before I get into the platform,
I want to be specific

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:38.220
about the problems it solves,

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:41.100
because if you're building
agents for the enterprise,

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:43.800
you've lived all three of these.

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:45.180
The first is the GUI gap.

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:47.550
Most enterprises'
work still happens

00:13:47.550 --> 00:13:48.990
in graphical applications

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:51.120
such as proprietary
line-of-business apps

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:52.680
or browser-based portals,

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:55.980
and these tools were built
for humans, not APIs.

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:58.080
Your agent needs to
interact with them.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:00.390
It needs a screen
and a way to use it.

00:14:00.390 --> 00:14:03.960
Windows 365 for Agents
gives it exactly that.

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:05.910
The second is the legacy gap.

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:09.810
Enterprises run line-of-business
applications that remain outside

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:11.880
of modernization efforts.

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:16.290
Mainframe terminal emulators,
desktop-only finance tools,

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:19.680
that internal app from 2008
that everyone depends on

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:21.600
and no one wants to rewrite.

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:24.840
Agents with Cloud PCs can
automate these systems

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:25.830
as they are.

00:14:25.830 --> 00:14:27.570
No API wrapping,

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:31.650
no screen-scraping hacks,
no rewrite required.

00:14:31.650 --> 00:14:33.720
And the last is the scale gap.

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:35.940
Maybe you've already solved
the first two problems

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:39.870
on a single VM somewhere, but
how do you go from one agent

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:42.960
on one machine to 100
agents serving your

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:44.550
entire organization?

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:48.540
How do you manage the images,
the security, the lifecycle?

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:50.670
That's what the
platform handles.

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:55.020
Pooled Cloud PCs managed
at scale, governed by IT,

00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:58.710
the same way you would manage
a fleet of employee machines.

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:01.290
So what is Windows
365 for Agents?

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:03.870
At its core, it's three things.

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:06.870
First, it's Cloud PCs
built for agents.

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:08.910
These are Windows environments.

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:12.630
Same Windows 365 Cloud PCs
that people use today,

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:17.190
but provisioned, connected,
and managed for agent use.

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:20.850
Your agent has a desktop, a
screen, a keyboard, a mouse,

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:24.900
and can access any application
that runs on Windows.

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:28.170
Second, it's an MCP tool layer.

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:31.230
MCP, the Model Context
Protocol, is the open standard

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:33.660
for connecting agents to tools.

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:35.520
We ship a set of MCP tools

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:38.970
that lets your agent provision
a Cloud PC, connect to it,

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:42.750
interact with it, and
manage its lifecycle.

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:45.300
If your agent
framework speaks MCP,

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:48.870
it works with Windows
365 for Agents.

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:53.580
Third, it's enterprise control
and operations you already know.

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:55.980
Cloud PC pools are
managed in Intune.

00:15:55.980 --> 00:15:58.350
Identity goes through Entra ID.

00:15:58.350 --> 00:15:59.760
Policies, compliance,

00:15:59.760 --> 00:16:02.160
conditional access,
it all just works.

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:04.170
There's nothing new
for IT to learn.

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:08.070
They manage agent Cloud PCs
the same way they manage user

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:09.360
Cloud PCs.

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:12.360
And all of this sits
within Agent 365.

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:16.800
The MCP tool is managed
with the Agent 365 portal,

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:19.740
and all of the observability
and the governance

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:24.270
for your Windows 365-enabled
agents surface the same way they

00:16:24.270 --> 00:16:28.740
would do for any Agent
365-enabled agent,

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:32.010
making it easy for your IT
admins to configure Windows 365

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:34.740
for Agents alongside
all of the other agents

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:36.900
in your enterprise tenant.

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:39.930
Now let's take a look at
how easy it is to integrate

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:46.240
with Windows 365 for Agents MCP
server using the A365 toolkit.

00:16:47.370 --> 00:16:49.110
This is the tooling manifest.

00:16:49.110 --> 00:16:52.050
It's where we declare which
MCP servers this agent has

00:16:52.050 --> 00:16:53.130
access to.

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:56.010
Right now, it's empty,
no servers configured,

00:16:56.010 --> 00:17:00.750
so the agent can't actually
access Windows 365 for Agents.

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:02.580
To see what's available
in the tenant,

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:06.990
I'll use the Agent 365 CLI
and run list available.

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:11.190
You can see there's a rich set
of MCP servers ready to go

00:17:11.190 --> 00:17:14.460
out of the box, available
through Agent 365.

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:17.550
The one we care about today is
the Windows 365 computer use

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:18.870
MCP server.

00:17:18.870 --> 00:17:21.600
Wiring it up is genuinely
a configuration change,

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:23.100
not a code rewrite.

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:27.260
I just run add MCP server
through the A365 CLI tool,

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:30.440
and it pulls the MCP
server into the manifest,

00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:33.140
and it writes all the
configuration the agent needs

00:17:33.140 --> 00:17:34.770
to authorize and use it.

00:17:34.770 --> 00:17:35.610
And there it is.

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:38.820
The manifest is updated, and
the agent now has access

00:17:38.820 --> 00:17:40.320
to that MCP server.

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:44.160
Now let me show you a real-world
use case of an AI agent running

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:46.470
on Windows 365 for Agents.

00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:48.500
The agent you see
here comes from one

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:51.230
of our lighthouse
customers, Simular.

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:53.720
Simular is a research and
product company focused

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:56.150
on achieving AGI
through building agents

00:17:56.150 --> 00:17:58.110
that work like humans.

00:17:58.110 --> 00:17:59.340
Earlier this year,

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:01.980
they introduced Cy,
an agentic coworker.

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:03.840
Cy sees the screen,
moves the mouse,

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:05.190
and types on the keyboard.

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:10.080
It also uses APIs,
terminals, and writes code.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:14.460
In this video, you will see Cy
using Windows 365 for Agents

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:17.460
to run an overnight claims
processing workflow

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:21.000
in a Contoso claims
app with no APIs.

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:25.500
Cy reads scanned claim
forms, extracts key fields,

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:28.890
verifies coverage, and
enters results directly

00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:34.580
through the UI inside a Windows
365 for Agents Cloud PC.

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:39.350
In short, Simular's Cy brings
the computer-use brain,

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:44.210
and Windows 365 for Agents
provides the secure execution

00:18:44.210 --> 00:18:46.710
layer for work to get done.

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:54.630
So let's bring it all together.

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:56.760
Three things to take away.

00:18:56.760 --> 00:18:59.550
First, stay focused
on agentic logic.

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:03.120
Windows 365 for Agents handles
the execution environment

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:04.950
underneath, so your time goes

00:19:04.950 --> 00:19:07.680
into what makes your
agent valuable.

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:12.510
Second, extend the reach of your
agents past the API boundary.

00:19:12.510 --> 00:19:14.910
A lot of enterprise
work still happens

00:19:14.910 --> 00:19:17.550
in line-of-business
applications that fall outside

00:19:17.550 --> 00:19:19.410
of modernization efforts.

00:19:19.410 --> 00:19:21.720
With a cloud PC, your
agent can operate them

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:23.760
in the current state.

00:19:23.760 --> 00:19:28.020
And third, build on one
streamlined Microsoft stack,

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:33.270
from the MCP server to Agent
365 to Intune and Entra.

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:35.220
Build, publish, govern,

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:40.580
and scale on the tools your
organization already trusts.

00:19:40.580 --> 00:19:44.330
If you want the full developer
cycle, Joydeep Mukherjee

00:19:44.330 --> 00:19:48.260
and Sam Shapiro have a deep
dive on-demand session

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:50.390
that takes you end-to-end.

00:19:50.390 --> 00:19:52.220
Go watch it next.

00:19:52.220 --> 00:19:53.150
Back to you, Roop.

00:19:53.150 --> 00:19:54.260
ROOP KIRAN CHEVURI:
Thank you, Phil.

00:19:54.260 --> 00:19:57.590
Developers and agents cannot
run without a secure foundation

00:19:57.590 --> 00:19:59.400
with centralized IT management.

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:01.080
We have looked at this
from the developer side,

00:20:01.080 --> 00:20:03.780
how quickly you can
get into a Cloud PC,

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:05.910
how the environment is
ready to code from start,

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:08.940
and how developers can get
the flexibility they need

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:12.690
without giving up a
consistent experience.

00:20:12.690 --> 00:20:16.740
We also saw how Windows 365 for
Agents extends this further,

00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:19.770
giving agents their
own Cloud PCs.

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:23.820
Full Windows environments where
they can see, interact with ops,

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:26.600
and operate much
like a person would.

00:20:26.600 --> 00:20:29.000
Now let's switch to the IT
admin perspective and look

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:33.050
at what makes all of that
possible behind the scenes.

00:20:33.050 --> 00:20:37.200
At its core, Windows 365 is
designed for simplicity.

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:39.960
IT can centrally provision
these environments,

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:43.380
choose the right images,
apply the right policies,

00:20:43.380 --> 00:20:45.690
and keep development
environments secure

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:49.140
and controlled without
slowing developers down.

00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:52.440
So while the developer
experience feels simple

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:55.320
and fast, it's still built
on an admin experience

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:58.470
that gives organizations the
governance, the flexibility,

00:20:58.470 --> 00:21:00.120
and security they need.

00:21:00.120 --> 00:21:01.740
Let's dive into more.

00:21:01.740 --> 00:21:03.600
From the IT admin side, this is

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:05.580
where you start tailoring
the experience based

00:21:05.580 --> 00:21:08.250
on what different
developers actually need.

00:21:08.250 --> 00:21:10.920
In the Intune portal,
there is a dedicated place

00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:13.710
to manage Cloud PCs,
apps, settings,

00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:15.360
and provisioning policies.

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:17.460
Let's start by creating
a provisioning policy

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:19.860
for my web apps dev team.

00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:23.550
This defines how and where
the Cloud PCs get created.

00:21:23.550 --> 00:21:27.030
Windows 365 supports
multiple workforce models.

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:29.550
So you can choose between
Windows 365 Enterprise,

00:21:29.550 --> 00:21:33.240
Windows 365 Flex Dedicated,
or Windows 365 Flex Shared,

00:21:33.240 --> 00:21:34.380
depending on the scenario,

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:37.020
whether a developer needs
a full-time Cloud PC

00:21:37.020 --> 00:21:40.680
or more flexible access
across environments.

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:43.710
My web app team needs
a full-time Cloud PC,

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:46.200
so I choose Enterprise here.

00:21:46.200 --> 00:21:49.320
On the networking side, you can
choose Microsoft Hosted Network

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:52.830
if you want a simpler, fully
Microsoft-managed setup,

00:21:52.830 --> 00:21:55.770
or Azure Network Connection if
you need Cloud PCs connected

00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:57.870
to your own Azure
virtual network.

00:21:57.870 --> 00:22:00.420
We recommend using
Microsoft Hosted Network

00:22:00.420 --> 00:22:03.750
for its enhanced resiliency,
so we'll select it here.

00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:06.240
And because Windows 365
supports deployment

00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:09.750
across regions globally, IT
can place Cloud PCs closer

00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:11.340
to the users for a
better experience.

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:14.880
My web app team works in US
West, so let's select it.

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:17.550
Images define the starting
point for every Cloud PC.

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:19.620
They determine the OS, tools,

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:22.080
and baseline a developer
gets on day one.

00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:24.780
So picking the
right image is key.

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:27.360
In the provisioning policy,
admins have two main parts,

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:30.270
marketplace images
or custom images.

00:22:30.270 --> 00:22:33.240
Marketplace images are the
fastest way to get started.

00:22:33.240 --> 00:22:36.480
For more control, you can
always use custom images,

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:39.900
including managed images or
Azure Compute Gallery images,

00:22:39.900 --> 00:22:41.880
all in the same experience.

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:44.700
With the Azure Compute
Gallery, you get versioned,

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:48.450
reusable images that are
easier to manage and scale.

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:51.840
For example, the test
ACG image is coming

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:53.220
from the Azure
Compute Gallery here,

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:56.190
while the others are
managed custom images.

00:22:56.190 --> 00:22:59.310
We also have this new
developer-ready marketplace

00:22:59.310 --> 00:23:02.400
image in preview, giving you
a coding-ready environment

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:04.140
out of the box with
the core tools

00:23:04.140 --> 00:23:06.390
across Windows and WSL Ubuntu.

00:23:06.390 --> 00:23:09.210
So developers can be productive
from the first sign-in.

00:23:09.210 --> 00:23:11.940
And if you need something
more specialized, as I said,

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:15.030
you can still layer in
custom or ACG images

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:18.660
to keep things consistent
and controlled.

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:22.650
Next, IT defines how the
environment is prepared.

00:23:22.650 --> 00:23:25.860
Device preparation policy
ensures apps, tools,

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:27.840
and scripts are in
place ahead of time,

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:30.090
so by the time the
Cloud PC is assigned,

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:32.850
it's not just created;
it's ready to use.

00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:35.610
The scope tags help
scale this across teams,

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:39.570
letting IT delegate access so
teams manage only what matters

00:23:39.570 --> 00:23:43.170
to them, while governance
still stays centralized.

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:46.500
I'll assign the web app
team manager scope tag here

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:50.760
with this scope tag that the
delegated role will only be able

00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:52.230
to access the resources

00:23:52.230 --> 00:23:56.250
that have the allowed scope
tag web app team manager.

00:23:56.250 --> 00:23:57.720
Resources with, let's say,

00:23:57.720 --> 00:24:00.210
backend developer scope
tag will not be accessible

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:02.100
to this delegated role.

00:24:02.100 --> 00:24:05.040
And once everything is set, you
assign the provisioning policy

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:07.740
to user group so the developers
get the right Cloud PC,

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:10.140
which is aligned to their
role, project, network,

00:24:10.140 --> 00:24:12.940
and the location
right from day one.

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:17.220
The next step you would want to
take is to implement security

00:24:17.220 --> 00:24:19.950
across these various
environments.

00:24:19.950 --> 00:24:22.830
Based on the client device
your developers are using,

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:26.250
you have an option to customize
the level of control.

00:24:26.250 --> 00:24:29.820
From a security perspective, you
may want the environment to be

00:24:29.820 --> 00:24:32.790
where only managed devices
are able to have access

00:24:32.790 --> 00:24:34.980
to the redirection controls.

00:24:34.980 --> 00:24:36.270
You would be able to do this

00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:37.830
through context-based
redirection,

00:24:37.830 --> 00:24:40.830
where you can configure
authentication context,

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:44.940
conditional access policies,
and Windows 365 settings

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:48.510
so that only managed compliant
devices will have drive,

00:24:48.510 --> 00:24:51.600
clipboard, printer, and
USB redirection enabled.

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:53.940
And if they don't meet
the compliance standards,

00:24:53.940 --> 00:24:56.640
those redirections are disabled.

00:24:56.640 --> 00:24:59.490
So let's enable
context-based redirection.

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:01.020
To enable context-based
redirection,

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:04.080
you first create an
authentication context in Intune

00:25:04.080 --> 00:25:07.470
and then apply it through a
conditional access policy.

00:25:07.470 --> 00:25:11.370
Here we select all users, choose
the authentication context,

00:25:11.370 --> 00:25:15.990
and required devices to
be marked as compliant.

00:25:15.990 --> 00:25:19.680
Next, we configure a Windows 365
Remote Connection Experience

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:23.100
setting and apply the same
authentication context

00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:25.530
to enforce these controls.

00:25:25.530 --> 00:25:27.000
Once this is complete,

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:30.510
only users with managed
devices will be granted access

00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:33.330
to redirection controls
between their physical device

00:25:33.330 --> 00:25:35.430
and the Cloud PC.

00:25:35.430 --> 00:25:37.980
You might be wondering
how IT management applies

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:41.220
to the AI agents running
on Windows 365 for Agents

00:25:41.220 --> 00:25:43.140
that Phil mentioned earlier.

00:25:43.140 --> 00:25:44.820
This is also done by Intune,

00:25:44.820 --> 00:25:48.060
providing one familiar
tool for both.

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:50.430
Inside the Intune Admin
Center, under Devices

00:25:50.430 --> 00:25:53.910
in Windows 365 Cloud PCs,
there's a dedicated experience

00:25:53.910 --> 00:25:57.510
for provisioning cloud PCs
specifically for agents.

00:25:57.510 --> 00:25:59.430
So I'll create a new
provisioning policy here.

00:25:59.430 --> 00:26:02.160
To get started, I'll add
a name and description

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:04.950
so I can easily
identify it later.

00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:07.590
I can associate a billing
policy, specify the number

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:11.070
of always available Cloud
PCs, and then add my agents.

00:26:11.070 --> 00:26:14.310
Here, I'll select two
agents and add them,

00:26:14.310 --> 00:26:16.950
and as more agents are
created, I'll have the ability

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:20.080
to add them to the existing
provisioning policies.

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:22.770
Next, I'll select an image.

00:26:22.770 --> 00:26:25.320
Today, I'll be using
a gallery image.

00:26:25.320 --> 00:26:26.880
Finally, I choose the language.

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:28.500
I'll stick with English.

00:26:28.500 --> 00:26:30.000
Here's all the
information associated

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:31.440
with my provisioning policy now,

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:35.310
and I can create this
with just one click.

00:26:35.310 --> 00:26:38.220
Now I have a pool of Cloud
PC for my agents to check

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:40.050
out and do the real work.

00:26:40.050 --> 00:26:43.590
Windows 365 makes it easy for IT
admins to centrally provision

00:26:43.590 --> 00:26:45.990
and manage developer
environments at scale.

00:26:45.990 --> 00:26:49.650
It offers flexibility, allowing
teams to tailor configurations,

00:26:49.650 --> 00:26:53.130
networking, and deployment
models to fit different needs.

00:26:53.130 --> 00:26:55.530
With standardized images
and automated setup,

00:26:55.530 --> 00:26:58.230
it delivers consistent,
ready-to-use environments,

00:26:58.230 --> 00:27:01.920
enabling IT admins to move
quickly while maintaining strong

00:27:01.920 --> 00:27:03.480
security and governance.

00:27:03.480 --> 00:27:05.490
Windows 365 enables
organizations

00:27:05.490 --> 00:27:07.980
to deliver flexible, secure
developer environments

00:27:07.980 --> 00:27:11.520
from anywhere, while giving
developers fast access

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:13.050
